WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.169 --> 00:00:04.889
Now, let's walk through the basic workflow of a project environment so that you can

2
00:00:04.889 --> 00:00:09.509
gain a better understanding of source control collaboration in action.

3
00:00:09.509 --> 00:00:12.800
First, let's meet our team.

4
00:00:12.800 --> 00:00:15.140
Developer A, an artist.

5
00:00:15.140 --> 00:00:18.149
Developer B, another artist.

6
00:00:18.149 --> 00:00:24.870
Their team lead, Developer C. And finally, the star of the show, the depot or server.

7
00:00:24.870 --> 00:00:31.550
Now, Developer A is super good at flowers, but not so much at sims, so it's time to

8
00:00:31.550 --> 00:00:35.950
team up with Developer B and get to work making the total flower package.

9
00:00:36.619 --> 00:00:40.259
First, Developer A creates a file locally.

10
00:00:40.259 --> 00:00:45.060
This is the flower asset that she's going to work on, but before she starts, she needs

11
00:00:45.060 --> 00:00:48.020
to add the file to the depot.

12
00:00:48.020 --> 00:00:52.060
This tells the depot to start tracking the flower and to include it on any submissions

13
00:00:52.060 --> 00:00:54.170
or updates.

14
00:00:54.170 --> 00:00:58.869
Developer A then works locally, and when she's finished working on the flower, she

15
00:00:58.869 --> 00:01:03.289
uploads the finished file to the depot with a description, submitting it, and creating

16
00:01:03.289 --> 00:01:04.769
a version.

17
00:01:04.769 --> 00:01:09.489
Now, Developer B gets updated and downloads the latest version from the depot locally

18
00:01:09.489 --> 00:01:11.260
to their machine.

19
00:01:11.260 --> 00:01:17.239
This gives Developer B an exact copy of the version that Developer A submitted.

20
00:01:17.239 --> 00:01:21.439
Developer B is now ready to do their thing and put that stem on the flower.

21
00:01:21.439 --> 00:01:27.359
So they start by checking out the flower, and that in turn locks the flower on the

22
00:01:27.359 --> 00:01:32.719
depot so that Developer B can start modifying the flower and add that stem.

23
00:01:32.719 --> 00:01:36.879
Now, checking out the file doesn't really prevent others from modifying the file, but

24
00:01:36.879 --> 00:01:39.549
it does prevent them from checking it out.

25
00:01:39.549 --> 00:01:42.989
This means that Developer B doesn't have to yell across the room to let Developer

26
00:01:42.989 --> 00:01:47.909
A know that they are working on the flower, so they really shouldn't try to change

27
00:01:47.909 --> 00:01:49.349
it.

28
00:01:49.349 --> 00:01:55.609
Developer B gets to work, and in no time, he has modified the flower and saved it locally.

29
00:01:55.609 --> 00:02:00.939
The stem is glorious, and the revision is ready to be submitted.

30
00:02:00.939 --> 00:02:04.319
So once again, the file is submitted and versioned.

31
00:02:04.319 --> 00:02:09.479
This new version now becomes the head revision or the latest revision for the flower.

32
00:02:09.479 --> 00:02:13.500
Once the flower is updated, the other team members will see that the file they have

33
00:02:13.500 --> 00:02:15.539
is now out of date.

34
00:02:15.539 --> 00:02:19.180
So if they try to modify it, they're going to be warned, and they'll need to jump through

35
00:02:19.180 --> 00:02:22.400
some hoops in order to save that file.

36
00:02:22.560 --> 00:02:26.960
Instead, the team will just update the copies that they've got locally.

37
00:02:26.960 --> 00:02:31.750
So Developer A will pull a new version and update her copy.

38
00:02:31.750 --> 00:02:36.389
Next, the team lead will pull the latest version for review, and he's happy with

39
00:02:36.389 --> 00:02:38.439
the results.

40
00:02:38.439 --> 00:02:43.319
Now the entire team is on the same page and ready to party.

41
00:02:43.319 --> 00:02:49.039
Okay, that's an ideal example, but let's say the team lead takes the flower to a director

42
00:02:49.039 --> 00:02:51.810
and they aren't happy.

43
00:02:51.810 --> 00:02:56.569
So the team repeats the process and goes through several notes to make multiple changes on

44
00:02:56.569 --> 00:03:01.689
the flower, pushing four or five iterations to the depot, eventually creating a version

45
00:03:01.689 --> 00:03:04.409
that results in a happy director.

46
00:03:04.409 --> 00:03:08.569
Unfortunately, the director, after seeing the results on camera, changes their mind

47
00:03:08.569 --> 00:03:11.930
and decides that where we ended up was not great.

48
00:03:11.930 --> 00:03:17.639
In fact, the director asks the team to go back to a second version, and it looks

49
00:03:17.639 --> 00:03:21.300
like that was the best version on screen.

50
00:03:21.300 --> 00:03:27.620
So in Perforce, you can revert to that in multiple ways, but the end result of reverting

51
00:03:27.620 --> 00:03:33.699
to the second iteration will actually create a sixth iteration, since the update to that

52
00:03:33.699 --> 00:03:38.500
file will be non-destructive to the other versions, just in case someone changes their

53
00:03:38.500 --> 00:03:41.020
mind one more time.

54
00:03:41.020 --> 00:03:43.620
That's it for the basic workflow and project environment.

55
00:03:43.620 --> 00:03:47.500
I hope this helps with your understanding of source control, and in our next section,

56
00:03:47.500 --> 00:03:52.139
we will explore the server and client setup as we look at working with the Perforce client.
